How to explain an implementation in an interview
For Principal Engineer interviews, a definition alone is rarely enough. Use the same four-part explanation for each term:
What: Define the concept.
How: Explain the component, framework, API, or algorithm used.
Why: Justify the engineering choice against alternatives.
Validation: Explain how you test correctness, security, scalability, and reliability.
For example, when asked about durable agent execution, you could say:
"I would implement durable execution using a PostgreSQL workflow store, explicit state transitions, and queue-based workers. Every completed step would be checkpointed. If a worker crashes, another worker would recover the workflow, reconcile uncertain external actions, and resume unfinished work. I would validate the design using fault-injection tests that terminate workers before and after external tool calls."
This demonstrates architecture, implementation, failure handling, and validation in one answer.
